<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stralsund</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stralsund is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Strelasund opposite the island of Rügen. Granted town rights in 1234 by Prince Wizlaw I of Rügen, it became one of the leading Hanseatic towns and gave its name to the Treaty of Stralsund of 1370, which marked the league's victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. It withstood Wallenstein's siege in 1628 and was Swedish until 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stralsund.jpg" alt="Stralsund" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moahim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WLM_-_2020_-_Stralsund_-_St._Nikolai_Kirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the area formed part of the Principality of Rügen, a Danish vassal principality ruled by a local West Slavic dynasty. In the course of German settlement many settlers and merchants came to the Strale settlement, and Prince Wizlaw I of Rügen granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law to the town in 1234. The town's success challenged Lübeck, which burned Stralsund down in 1249; it was rebuilt with a wall with 11 gates and 30 watchtowers. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1293, and in the 14th century 300 ships flew its flag in the Baltic. In 1325 the principality became part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, though Stralsund kept considerable independence.</p><p>In 1628, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, Imperial forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> besieged the city after the council refused the Capitulation of Franzburg. Stralsund resisted with Danish and Swedish support. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 made it part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a>. It was lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1678 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1679. In the Great Northern War Charles XII led the defence of the city for a year in 1715, and Swedish control lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army occupied it in 1807. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815. Allied bombing in 1944 killed some 800 people and destroyed about 8,000 dwellings, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied the city on 28 April 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Stralsund is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>, on the Strelasund opposite the island of Rügen, to which the Strelasund Crossing connects it. In 2002 its old town and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> were listed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> as the Historic Centres of Stralsund and Wismar, for their Brick Gothic buildings and their importance in the Hanseatic period and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. They include the Gothic city hall, St Nicholas' Church, built in 1270–1360, and St Mary's Church, built in 1383–1473, whose octagonal tower is 104 metres high.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stralsund” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The coastal towns became members of the Hanseatic League, trading grain, timber, herring and beer across the Baltic; <b>Stralsund</b> was one of the leading Hanse cities, and the Peace of Stralsund of 1370 with Denmark marked the height of the League’s power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They formed a sworn association on Gotland, trading through Visby to Novgorod, and new German towns such as Riga, Reval (Tallinn), Danzig (Gdańsk), Rostock, Wismar and <b>Stralsund</b> were founded along the southern and eastern coasts, many of them under Lübeck law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the north, German trading towns led by Lübeck formed the Hanseatic League, whose victory over Valdemar IV of Denmark, sealed by the Treaty of <b>Stralsund</b> in 1370, marked the height of its power.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Power was shared with the estates and with Hanseatic towns such as <b>Stralsund</b>, Greifswald and Anklam, which grew rich on Baltic trade, and Greifswald University was founded in 1456.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast">Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast</a></li><li><blockquote>The princes held the island and a strip of the Pomeranian mainland, where Wizlaw I granted <b>Stralsund</b> town rights in 1234.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a></li><li><blockquote>When the last Pomeranian duke died in 1637, Sweden kept control, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 granted it Western Pomerania with Stettin, <b>Stralsund</b> and the island of Rügen, held as an imperial fief.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a></li></ul></section>
